{
    "Title": "Model Card",
    "Model Details": {
        "Name": "RINE",
        "Overview": "The RINE model is designed for synthetic image detection. It leverages representations from intermediate encoder-blocks of CLIP to identify synthetic images. By mapping these representations to a learnable forgery-aware vector space and incorporating the importance of each intermediate Transformer block using a Trainable Importance Estimator (TIE) module, the model achieves exceptional generalization. It outperforms state-of-the-art methods by an average of +10.6% in performance improvement. The model is trained on synthetic image datasets generated by ProGAN, considering 1, 2, and 4 object classes for training, and evaluated on 20 test datasets including images from various generative models like GAN, Diffusion, deepfake, low-level vision, perceptual loss, and DALL-E.",
        "Version": "1.0",
        "License": "Apache-2.0",
        "References": {
            "github": "https://github.com/mever-team/rine",
            "DOI": "arXiv:2402.19091v2"
        }
    },
    "Considerations": {
        "Use Case": "Synthetic Image Detection",
        "Limitations": {},
        "Ethical Considerations": {}
    },
    "Training": {
        "Text": "The model is trained on synthetic image datasets using image representations from intermediate Transformer blocks of CLIP's image-encoder. Training is conducted on images generated by ProGAN, considering 1, 2, and 4 object classes. Specifically, the best models (1-class, 2-class, 4-class) are re-trained for only one epoch using 20%, 50%, 80%, and 100% of the training data."
    },
    "Testing": {
        "Text": "The model is evaluated on 20 test datasets, including images from various generative models like GAN, Diffusion, deepfake, low-level vision, perceptual loss, and DALL-E, to assess its performance in detecting synthetic images. The performance on the test datasets is presented in figures showing ACC and AP for different architectures of the trainable part of the model."
    },
    "Quantitative Analysis": {
        "Text": "The RINE model demonstrates an average +10.6% absolute performance improvement over state-of-the-art methods when tested on 20 datasets. It achieves this level of performance with just 6.3M learnable parameters, trained for only 1 epoch (~8 minutes). The model's robustness to common image perturbations is evaluated, showing high robustness against cropping and compression, with minimal performance losses, and a good level of robustness against blurring and noise addition. The importance of intermediate stages in the model is highlighted, emphasizing the significance of each block in the model's performance."
    }
}